<exercisegroup cols="2">
<title>Cofactor and adjoint matrices</title>

<introduction><p>
	For each matrix:
	<ol>
		<li> Compute the associated cofactor matrix.            </li>
		<li> Compute the associated adjoint matrix.             </li>
		<li> Compute the product of the matrix and its adjoint. </li>
		<li>
			If the matrix is invertible,
			use the <xref ref="theorem-more-det-adjoint-inversion-formula" text="title" /> formula
			(<xref ref="theorem-more-det-adjoint-inversion-formula" />)
			to compute its inverse.
		</li>
	</ol>
</p></introduction>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle
		\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
			-3 \amp  0 \amp 5 \\
			-3 \amp  0 \amp 2 \\
			 5 \amp -1 \amp 5
		\end{abmatrix}
	</m></p></statement>

	<answer><p><ol>

		<li><p><m>\displaystyle
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
				 2 \amp  25 \amp  3 \\
				-5 \amp -40 \amp -3 \\
				 0 \amp  -9 \amp  0
			\end{abmatrix}
		</m></p></li>

		<li><p><m>\displaystyle
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
				 2 \amp  -5 \amp  0 \\
				25 \amp -40 \amp -9 \\
				 3 \amp  -3 \amp  0
			\end{abmatrix}
		</m></p></li>

		<li><p><m>\displaystyle
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
				-3 \amp  0 \amp 5 \\
				-3 \amp  0 \amp 2 \\
				 5 \amp -1 \amp 5
			\end{abmatrix}
				\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
					 2 \amp  -5 \amp  0 \\
					25 \amp -40 \amp -9 \\
					 3 \amp  -3 \amp  0
			\end{abmatrix}
			=
			\begin{bmatrix}
				9 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
				0 \amp 9 \amp 0 \\
				0 \amp 0 \amp 9
			\end{bmatrix}
		</m></p></li>

		<li><p>
			The determinant of the original matrix is <m>9</m>,
			so the matrix is invertible with inverse
			<md>
				\frac{1}{9} \,
				\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
					 2 \amp  -5 \amp  0 \\
					25 \amp -40 \amp -9 \\
					 3 \amp  -3 \amp  0
				\end{abmatrix}
			</md>.
		</p></li>

	</ol></p></answer>
</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle
		\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
			-3 \amp 5 \amp 0 \\
			-4 \amp 2 \amp 4 \\
			 4 \amp 2 \amp 5
		\end{abmatrix}
	</m></p></statement>

	<answer><p><ol>

		<li><p><m>\displaystyle
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
				  2 \amp  36 \amp -16 \\
				-25 \amp -15 \amp  26 \\
				 20 \amp  12 \amp  14
			\end{abmatrix}
		</m></p></li>

		<li><p><m>\displaystyle
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
				  2 \amp -25 \amp 20 \\
				 36 \amp -15 \amp 12 \\
				-16 \amp  26 \amp 14
			\end{abmatrix}
		</m></p></li>

		<li><p><m>\displaystyle
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
				-3 \amp 5 \amp 0 \\
				-4 \amp 2 \amp 4 \\
				 4 \amp 2 \amp 5
			\end{abmatrix}
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
				  2 \amp -25 \amp 20 \\
				 36 \amp -15 \amp 12 \\
				-16 \amp  26 \amp 14
			\end{abmatrix}
			=
			\begin{bmatrix}
				174 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
				0 \amp 174 \amp 0 \\
				0 \amp 0 \amp 174
			\end{bmatrix}
		</m></p></li>

		<li><p>
			The determinant of the original matrix is <m>174</m>,
			so the matrix is invertible with inverse
			<md>
				\frac{1}{174} \,
				\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
					  2 \amp -25 \amp 20 \\
					 36 \amp -15 \amp 12 \\
					-16 \amp  26 \amp 14
				\end{abmatrix}
			</md>.
		</p></li>

	</ol></p></answer>
</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle
		\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
			 5 \amp -3 \amp  8 \\
			 1 \amp  3 \amp -2 \\
			-5 \amp  4 \amp -9
		\end{abmatrix}
	</m></p></statement>

	<answer><p><ol>

		<li><p><m>\displaystyle
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
				-19 \amp 19 \amp 19 \\
				  5 \amp -5 \amp -5 \\
				-18 \amp 18 \amp 18
			\end{abmatrix}
		</m></p></li>

		<li><p><m>\displaystyle
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
				-19 \amp  5 \amp -18 \\
				 19 \amp -5 \amp  18 \\
				 19 \amp -5 \amp  18
			\end{abmatrix}
		</m></p></li>

		<li><p><m>\displaystyle
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
				 5 \amp -3 \amp  8 \\
				 1 \amp  3 \amp -2 \\
				-5 \amp  4 \amp -9
			\end{abmatrix}
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
				-19 \amp  5 \amp -18 \\
				 19 \amp -5 \amp  18 \\
				 19 \amp -5 \amp  18
			\end{abmatrix}
			=
			\zerovec
		</m></p></li>

		<li><p>
			The determinant of the original matrix is <m>0</m>,
			so it is not invertible.
			Without computing the determinant,
			we can also see that the adjoint is not invertible because its RREF would have two rows of zeros.
			As an invertible matrix would have an invertible adjoint,
			the original matrix cannot be invertible if its adjoint is not invertible.
		</p></li>

	</ol></p></answer>
</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle
		\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
			-5 \amp 2 \amp -3 \\
			 0 \amp 5 \amp -2 \\
			 0 \amp 0 \amp  4
		\end{abmatrix}
	</m></p></statement>

	<answer><p><ol>

		<li><p><m>\displaystyle
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
				20 \amp   0 \amp   0 \\
				-8 \amp -20 \amp   0 \\
				11 \amp -10 \amp -25
			\end{abmatrix}
		</m></p></li>

		<li><p><m>\displaystyle
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
				20 \amp - 8 \amp  11 \\
				 0 \amp -20 \amp -10 \\
				 0 \amp   0 \amp -25
			\end{abmatrix}
		</m></p></li>

		<li><p><m>\displaystyle
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
				-5 \amp 2 \amp -3 \\
				 0 \amp 5 \amp -2 \\
				 0 \amp 0 \amp  4
			\end{abmatrix}
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
				20 \amp - 8 \amp  11 \\
				 0 \amp -20 \amp -10 \\
				 0 \amp   0 \amp -25
			\end{abmatrix}
			=
			\begin{bmatrix}
				-100 \amp   0  \amp   0  \\
				  0  \amp -100 \amp   0  \\
				  0  \amp   0  \amp -100
			\end{bmatrix}
		</m></p></li>

		<li><p>
			The determinant of the original matrix is <m>-100</m>,
			so the matrix is invertible with inverse
			<md>
				- \frac{1}{100} \,
				\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
					20 \amp - 8 \amp  11 \\
					 0 \amp -20 \amp -10 \\
					 0 \amp   0 \amp -25
				\end{abmatrix}
			</md>.
		</p></li>

	</ol></p></answer>
</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle
		\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
			 1 \amp  0 \amp  0 \\
			-5 \amp -4 \amp  0 \\
			 3 \amp  1 \amp -5
		\end{abmatrix}
	</m></p></statement>

	<answer><p><ol>

		<li><p><m>\displaystyle
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
				20 \amp -25 \amp  7 \\
				 0 \amp - 5 \amp -1 \\
				 0 \amp   0 \amp -4
			\end{abmatrix}
		</m></p></li>

		<li><p><m>\displaystyle
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
				 20 \amp  0 \amp  0 \\
				-25 \amp -5 \amp  0 \\
				  7 \amp -1 \amp -4
			\end{abmatrix}
		</m></p></li>

		<li><p><m>\displaystyle
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
				 1 \amp  0 \amp  0 \\
				-5 \amp -4 \amp  0 \\
				 3 \amp  1 \amp -5
			\end{abmatrix}
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
				 20 \amp  0 \amp  0 \\
				-25 \amp -5 \amp  0 \\
				  7 \amp -1 \amp -4
			\end{abmatrix}
			=
			\begin{bmatrix}
				20 \amp  0 \amp  0 \\
				 0 \amp 20 \amp  0 \\
				 0 \amp  0 \amp 20
			\end{bmatrix}
		</m></p></li>

		<li><p>
			The determinant of the original matrix is <m>20</m>,
			so the matrix is invertible with inverse
			<md>
				\frac{1}{20} \,
				\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
					 20 \amp  0 \amp  0 \\
					-25 \amp -5 \amp  0 \\
					  7 \amp -1 \amp -4
				\end{abmatrix}
			</md>.
		</p></li>

	</ol></p></answer>
</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle
		\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
			 3 \amp 0 \amp -1 \\
			 0 \amp 5 \amp  4 \\
			-1 \amp 4 \amp  2
		\end{abmatrix}
	</m></p></statement>

	<answer><p><ol>

		<li><p><m>\displaystyle
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
				-6 \amp  -4 \amp   5 \\
				-4 \amp   5 \amp -12 \\
				 5 \amp -12 \amp  15
			\end{abmatrix}
		</m></p></li>

		<li><p>
			Notice that the matrix of cofactors is symmetric,
			hence the adjoint matrix is equal to the matrix of cofactors in this case.
		</p></li>

		<li><p><m>\displaystyle
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
				 3 \amp 0 \amp -1 \\
				 0 \amp 5 \amp  4 \\
				-1 \amp 4 \amp  2
			\end{abmatrix}
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
				-6 \amp  -4 \amp   5 \\
				-4 \amp   5 \amp -12 \\
				 5 \amp -12 \amp  15
			\end{abmatrix}
			=
			\begin{bmatrix}
				-23 \amp   0 \amp   0 \\
				  0 \amp -23 \amp   0 \\
				  0 \amp   0 \amp -23
			\end{bmatrix}
		</m></p></li>

		<li><p>
			The determinant of the original matrix is <m>-23</m>,
			so the matrix is invertible with inverse
			<md>
				- \frac{1}{23} \,
				\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
					-6 \amp  -4 \amp   5 \\
					-4 \amp   5 \amp -12 \\
					 5 \amp -12 \amp  15
				\end{abmatrix}
			</md>.
		</p></li>

	</ol></p></answer>
</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle
		\begin{abmatrix}{rrrr}
			 0 \amp  0 \amp 0 \amp  1 \\
			 1 \amp  1 \amp 0 \amp  2 \\
			-2 \amp -1 \amp 0 \amp  5 \\
			 0 \amp  0 \amp 1 \amp -4
		\end{abmatrix}
	</m></p></statement>

	<answer><p><ol>

		<li><p><m>\displaystyle
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrrr}
				-7 \amp  9 \amp -4 \amp -1 \\
				 1 \amp -2 \amp  0 \amp  0 \\
				 1 \amp -1 \amp  0 \amp  0 \\
				 0 \amp  0 \amp -1 \amp  0
			\end{abmatrix}
		</m></p></li>

		<li><p><m>\displaystyle
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrrr}
				-7 \amp  1 \amp  1 \amp  0 \\
				 9 \amp -2 \amp -1 \amp  0 \\
				-4 \amp  0 \amp  0 \amp -1 \\
				-1 \amp  0 \amp  0 \amp  0
			\end{abmatrix}
		</m></p></li>

		<li><p><m>\displaystyle
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrrr}
				 0 \amp  0 \amp 0 \amp  1 \\
				 1 \amp  1 \amp 0 \amp  2 \\
				-2 \amp -1 \amp 0 \amp  5 \\
				 0 \amp  0 \amp 1 \amp -4
			\end{abmatrix}
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrrr}
				-7 \amp  1 \amp  1 \amp  0 \\
				 9 \amp -2 \amp -1 \amp  0 \\
				-4 \amp  0 \amp  0 \amp -1 \\
				-1 \amp  0 \amp  0 \amp  0
			\end{abmatrix}
			=
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrrr}
				-1 \amp  0 \amp  0 \amp  0 \\
				 0 \amp -1 \amp  0 \amp  0 \\
				 0 \amp  0 \amp -1 \amp  0 \\
				 0 \amp  0 \amp  0 \amp -1
			\end{abmatrix}
		</m></p></li>

		<li><p>
			The determinant of the original matrix is <m>-1</m>,
			so the matrix is invertible with inverse
			<md>
				\begin{abmatrix}{rrrr}
					 7 \amp -1 \amp -1 \amp 0 \\
					-9 \amp  2 \amp  1 \amp 0 \\
					 4 \amp  0 \amp  0 \amp 1 \\
					 1 \amp  0 \amp  0 \amp 0
				\end{abmatrix}
			</md>.
		</p></li>

	</ol></p></answer>
</exercise>

</exercisegroup>
